  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSunday finally brings the transition day to drier weather as the \npesky upper low that's been spinning around New England kicks off to \nthe east. As it exits stage right, it'll throw one more shortwave \ndown through the Great Lakes late morning to early afternoon. Low-\nlevel flow will veer to more north-northwesterly and shift the axis \nof lake-effect showers farther southwest into eastern OH and the WV \nlower elevations. The Huron connection looks to break off and the \nmoisture fetch source shifts to the minor axis of Lake Erie, all \nwhile a lowering subsidence inversion from building high \npressure gradually cuts off the depth of the moisture. REFS \nprobability of precip focuses the footprint of rain showers \nbetween ZZV and HLG by late morning, and with the shortwave \npassage, it should taper off and return dry weather to the vast \nmajority of the area.\n\nThe exception to that will be the PA & WV ridges where some upslope \nenhancement will continue into this evening. Poor depth to the \nmoisture column lends to inefficient snow crystal growth, but \nthere's about a 40-60% chance of another tenth of an inch with much \nlower probs for half an inch around 10-20%. Most of that would come \nfor the PA ridges early Sunday and the WV ridges late Sunday outside \nof the warmest part of the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nUpper troughing exits on Monday as the surface high eventually \nbecomes the dominant feature while mid-level ridging establishes. \nThere's quite notable agreement among all ensemble solutions \nfavoring this pattern keeping stronghold through most of the week.\nDry weather returns across the board with high confidence, and \ndaytime highs will moderate back to above-normal values within weak \nsoutherly flow. We will see some decent diurnal temperature swings, \nthough, as light wind and clearing skies allow for efficient \nradiational cooling and a good probability of a couple consecutive \nnights of lows in the 20s. But, by the time late week comes around, \nNBM probs for highs >60 degrees ramp up quite high. We'll have to \nlook toward sometime next weekend to see the next potential for rain \nto return as ridging breaks down, but that's when we start to \nintroduce some disagreement with how much the ridge breaks down/how \nfar east a Midwest trough can penetrate.",
